    "product_id": "17404af6-085b-44ac-b88b-a1ec2e09cc31",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 110916\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n216 AM MST Sun Oct 11 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms will continue to be\n  possible for portions of the area today as the remnants of \n  Tropical Cyclone Rachel lifts northward into Nevada. Locally \n  heavy rainfall will be possible along with breezy to windy \n  conditions.\n\n- Additional chances for mostly light rain will continue for\n  portions of the region, especially across South Central Arizona\n  Monday into Tuesday.\n  \n- Below normal temperatures are anticipated through the middle of\n  the week with a drier and quiet weather pattern settling into\n  the region by Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nThe remnants of TC Rachel are quickly lifting northward across \nsouthern California early this morning with moderate to heavy \nrainfall diminishing from south to north. Localized flash \nflooding will continue to be a concern over the next few hours \nbefore much of the rain moves out of the area by sunrise. Isolated\nto scattered showers and thunderstorms are also expected early\nthis morning across south-central Arizona with a broken line \ncurrently over western Pima and Maricopa Counties drifting \nnortheastward. \n\nStrong gusty winds have also developed across portions of the \narea with gusts as high as 50-55 mph across Imperial and Yuma \nCounties since midnight. Wind gusts of 40-50 mph should be quite \ncommon through sunrise across southeast California and southwest \nArizona. The windy conditions will eventually shift eastward \nthrough south-central Arizona later this morning with gusts of \n35-45 mph developing. Additional showers and isolated \nthunderstorms should develop across south-central Arizona by this \nafternoon with a focus more across the high terrain driven by \nstrong south southwesterly upslope flow. The remnants of Rachel \nwill be completely out of our area by this afternoon, but we will \nremain under the influence of a large Pacific trough and broad \nupper level ascent going into Monday. A break in the shower \nactivity is expected tonight with only widely isolated showers \npossible. Monday should be much of the same with upslope forcing \nwaining and drier air gradually working in from the southwest \nahead of the main shortwave. We expect some occasional isolated \nshowers and maybe a few thunderstorms on Monday, but overall not \nmuch additional rainfall for most areas. \n\nRain chances are likely to improve again going into Monday night\nas guidance shows the main shortwave clipping our area as it\ntracks to the northeast, as well as a weak cold front moving \nacross the area. Hi-res CAMs are beginning to show some shower \nactivity developing across the western deserts as early as Monday \nafternoon before pushing into Arizona Monday evening into Monday \nnight. Given that moisture is expected to be fairly limited, \nrainfall amounts should be light with lower desert areas mostly \nless than 0.10\" to at most 0.25-0.50\" over the high terrain north\nand northeast of Phoenix. \n\nThe temperature forecast likely brings some good news with a\nsubstantially cooler air mass settling in over the region.\nDaytime temperatures today will barely budge with afternoon highs\nonly reaching into the low 80s across the western deserts to\naround 85 degrees in the Phoenix area. Further cooling is\nanticipated for Monday with highs in the upper 70s to the lower\n80s, even before the cold front moves through the area. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nEnsembles are in good agreement the Pacific low will slowly drift\neast northeastward across far northern Arizona and southern Utah\non Tuesday before most of the energy exits to the northeast on \nWednesday. Additional dry air will spread across the region from \nthe northwest during this time with PWATs dropping to 75% of \nnormal and skies clearing out later on Tuesday. Behind the cold \nfront on Tuesday, temperatures will dip further with NBM forecast \nhighs only in the mid 70s Tuesday, or 10-15 degrees below normal. \nOvernight lows Tuesday night/Wednesday morning are also expected \nto fall well into the 50s for the bulk of the area to as cool as \nthe mid to upper 40s for rural lower desert and higher terrain \nlocations. \n\nModel guidance is still somewhat uncertain with the eventual\nplacement of the incoming upper level ridge and a likely lingering\ntrough axis and/or cut-off low that should form late week. No\nmatter the eventual evolution, our weather will basically stay the\nsame with dry conditions prevailing late week into next weekend.\nTemperatures will moderate some late week with highs returning\ninto the 80s, but likely staying below normal. We may end up \nhaving another back-door cool front moving into the area from the \neast by Friday or Saturday and this should either maintain or cool\noff temperatures going into next weekend. We should also have \nanother period of gusty easterly winds develop across eastern and \nsouth-central Arizona by next Friday or Saturday.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 0615Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nTwo bands of showers and storms are expected to move through\novernight/early tomorrow morning. The first band is expected to\nmove through between 09-11Z and the second band between 14-16Z.\nCigs are expected as low as 5 kft, with FEW-SCT cloud bases as low\nas 3 kft with any thunderstorms. The heavy rain could also reduce\nVIS down to 2-4 SM. After rain ends tomorrow morning dry\nconditions are expected the remainder of the TAF period. Then, the\nhigh wind gusts will be the main concern. Wind directions will\nfavor the E/SE through tomorrow morning becoming more southerly\nby late tomorrow morning/tomorrow afternoon. The gusty winds will\nstart tomorrow mid-to-late morning with gusts of 25-35 kt\nexpected. This could lead to areas of lofted/blowing dust causing\nreduced VIS. Wind gusts will taper off around sunset, returning to\n8-12 kt. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nThere will be lingering VCSH/-TSRA through the overnight hours at\nboth terminals. Cigs will be as low as 5 kft with any thunderstorm\nactivity, otherwise they will be aoa 10 kft. Wind directions at\nKIPL will favor the NE overnight before going SW early in the\nmorning then westerly tomorrow evening. At KBLH, current NNE winds\nwill go southerly early tomorrow morning then more SW'rly tomorrow\nevening. Wind speeds overnight will generally be around 8-12 kt,\nwith some occasional gusts in the 20s possible with any\nthunderstorms. Then windy conditions will develop by mid-morning\nwith gusts of 25-30 kt expected with some gusts upwards of 35-40\nkt possible at time through the afternoon. This would likely lead\nto BLDU and reduced VIS. Wind speeds will then return to 8-13 kt\nin the evening. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nActive weather will continue through the early part of the week\nwith the remnants of Tropical Cyclone Rachel affecting the area\ntoday followed by trough of low pressure for Monday and Tuesday.\nPeriods of rain and windy conditions will affect the area today \nwith locally moderate to heavy rainfall possible. More scattered \nshower and isolated thunderstorm activity is then anticipated for \nMonday and Tuesday with a focus over the Arizona high terrain. \nHumidities will remain very elevated through Tuesday with MinRH \nvalues mostly between 40-60%, while temperatures fall to well\nbelow normal. Drier conditions will gradually settle back into the\nregion during the latter half of the week with rain chances ending\nby Wednesday.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Wind Advisory until 5 PM MST Sunday for AZZ530>533-535-536.\n\n     Wind Advisory from 8 AM to 5 PM MST Sunday for AZZ534-537>563.\n\nCA...Wind Advisory until 5 PM PDT Sunday for CAZ560>570.\n\n     Flood Watch through Sunday afternoon for CAZ560-561-568.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Kuhlman\nLONG TERM...Kuhlman\nAVIATION...Berislavich\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman\n",
